{:edb/db-before-t 1247 :edb/db-after-t 1248 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534560 50 #inst "2026-10-12T03:04:09.348Z" 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186159494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159495 13194139534560 17592186045417 false] [17592186159494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159496 13194139534560 17592186045417 false] [17592186159494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159497 13194139534560 17592186045417 false] [17592186159494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159498 13194139534560 17592186045417 false] [17592186159494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159499 13194139534560 17592186045417 false] [17592186159494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425735 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186159494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425739 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186159494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425747 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186159494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425748 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186159494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425749 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186159494 1078 #edb/ref 17592186425739 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186159566 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159571 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186162107 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425753 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186162107 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425754 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186162108 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425755 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186162382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186162382 1071 "A sample $X_{1}, X_{2}, X_{3}$ of three nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $5cm$ and $10cm$, distributed in the ratio $4: 1$. Calculate the standard error of $X^(―)$. Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534560 17592186045417 false] [17592186162382 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ of three nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{10}\\,\\text{cm},$ distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186162382 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the nails in the population have lengths $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{10}\\,\\text{cm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $4+1=5$ nails only with the following lengths:\n\n$$\n5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm}\\,10\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot 5+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(20+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{5} \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{cm},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot {5}^{2}+{10}^{2})-{6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(200)-36 \\\\  & =40-36 \\\\  & =4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{4}{3}\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}}≈1.15\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186162394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186162394 1071 "A sample $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{n}$ of $n$ employees is drawn from a population. The population has employees with two different working hours, $38h/week$ and $40h/week$, distributed in the ratio $3: 1$. Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.5h/week$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534560 17592186045417 false] [17592186162394 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ employees is drawn from a population. The population has employees with two different working hours, $38\\,\\text{h/week}$ and $40\\,\\text{h/week},$ distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than  $0.5\\,\\text{h/week}.$" 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186162394 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the employees in the population have working hours $38\\,\\text{h/week}$ and $40\\,\\text{h/week},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $3+1=4$ employees only with the following working hours:\n\n$$\n38\\,\\text{h/week},\\,38\\,\\text{h/week},\\,38\\,\\text{h/week},\\,40\\,\\text{h/week}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot 38+40) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(114+40) \\\\  & =\\frac{154}{4} \\\\  & =38.5\\,\\text{h/week},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot {38}^{2}+{40}^{2})-{38.5}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(5\\,932)-1\\,482.25 \\\\  & =1\\,483-1\\,482.25 \\\\  & =0.75\\,{(\\text{h/week})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.75},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.5\\,\\text{h/week}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{n}} & <0.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{0.75}}{0.5} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{{0.5}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{0.25}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{3} \\\\ n & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=4.$" 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425729 1043 #edb/ref 17592186425756 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425729 1043 #edb/ref 17592186425757 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425729 1043 #edb/ref 17592186425758 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425729 1043 #edb/ref 17592186425759 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425729 1043 #edb/ref 17592186425760 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425729 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425729 1045 "Variance of Sample Means" 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425729 1046 #edb/ref 17592186425756 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425729 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425729 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049674 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425729 1053 #uuid "7fa56d66-b299-55c7-8a43-47f002ff063b" 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425730 1080 "0.17" 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425730 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425730 1083 #uuid "0a2ebf1d-6b21-517e-8473-6b5520ced24c" 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425731 1080 "197" 13194139534560 17592186045417 true] [17592186425731 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534560 175921860